; lego_pf_tx.loom
; LEGO Power Functions infrared transmitter on OUT0, fed by the host through
; INQ[0]: one word per message, the LRC computed here, the five
; transmissions with their channel-dependent spacing.
;
; Written on 2026-11-07, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035): no design document names LEGO
; Power Functions. Nothing in Loom was built for infrared: there is no PWM
; and no carrier generator. The program makes the 38 kHz carrier itself, as
; ir_nec_tx.loom does, with two pieces of hardware built for other jobs: the
; deadline-latched pin write (`SETP pin, v, D`, SEMANTICS 6.10, D-016),
; built so that WS2812 pulses land on the exact clock, and the fractional
; tick divider (TICK_FRAC, SEMANTICS 4), built for UART rates. Power
; Functions codes a bit in the time from one 6-cycle mark to the next, where
; NEC codes the space after a 22-cycle mark and SIRC the mark: here that is
; only a different WAITD after the same burst. What the firmware does that
; no hardware does: the carrier, the pulse-distance code, the LRC, the five
; transmissions spaced by the channel, the hold stream and the pre-emption
; by a new command. It needs the FIFOs (POP, WAITB INQ_NE) and the latch.
;
; Pin: LED = OUT0, push-pull, high = LED on, through a transistor (a pad
; cannot drive an IR LED's 50..100 mA itself). Idle low: the LED is off.
;
; Host command format (FIFO space, thread 0), one word per command:
;   bits 15:4  nibbles 1, 2 and 3 of the message exactly as they go on the
;              wire (toggle or address, escape, channel, address, mode,
;              data: the host owns every field, the toggle included, because
;              combo PWM mode has no toggle bit, LEGO document section
;              "Combo PWM mode");
;   bits 3:0   not sent: the program puts the LRC there. Bit 0 is HOLD: after
;              the fifth transmission the message goes on at the spacing of
;              the fourth to the fifth until the next word comes (a key held
;              down, section "Transmitting Messages"). Bits 3:1 are ignored.
; Nothing is ever pushed to OUTQ.
;
; Wire format (LEGO Power Functions RC, v1.20, 26.02.2010). Section "LEGO
; Power Functions RC Encoding": a mark is six cycles of 38 kHz; a bit is
; timed from the start of its mark to the start of the next mark: a low bit
; 16 cycles (421 us), a high bit 27 (711 us), the start and stop bits 45
; (1184 us). Section "LEGO Power Functions RC Protocol": a message is the
; start bit, 16 bits (nibble 1, nibble 2, nibble 3, LRC, each most
; significant bit first) and the stop bit; LRC = 0xF xor nibble 1 xor nibble
; 2 xor nibble 3. So a message is 18 marks: the start mark, one mark before
; each data bit, and the stop mark, which ends the last data bit.
;
; Retransmission (section "Transmitting Messages"). With tm = 16 ms, the
; maximum message length, and Ch the channel 1..4 (CC + 1): the first
; message after (4 - Ch) tm, the next two 5 tm start to start, the
; following ones (6 + 2 Ch) tm start to start; five in all, and while a key
; is held the message goes on at the last spacing. A new command pre-empts
; the copies still to come ("five exactly matching messages, if no other
; buttons are pressed or released"). Here tm = 608 carrier cycles, exactly
; 16 ms, so every start is on the carrier grid:
;   Ch  first delay   1-2, 2-3     3-4, 4-5 and the hold stream
;   1   3 tm  48 ms   5 tm 80 ms    8 tm 128 ms
;   2   2 tm  32 ms                10 tm 160 ms
;   3   1 tm  16 ms                12 tm 192 ms
;   4   0                          14 tm 224 ms
; The first delay counts from the word's arrival plus K = 4 cycles (105 us)
; of set-up; the starts after it are exact. A word queued while copies are
; still to come is checked LEAD ticks before each copy's start: it ends the
; sequence there and its own first delay starts. The first message of every
; word always goes out.
;
; Tick (set by the host before RUN, the M2 convention: the clock is the
; board's): one tick is a third of a carrier period, TICK = f_clk / 114 kHz.
;   50 MHz: TICK_INT 438, TICK_FRAC 153: 438.59765625 clocks (ideal
;     438.5965, +2.7 ppm): 37,999.90 Hz, every edge within one clock of the
;     ideal grid.
;   1.824 MHz (the RTL tests): TICK_INT 16, TICK_FRAC 0: exactly 38 kHz.
; The deadline schedule is proved for ticks of 16 clocks or more (.tick).
;
; Duty 1/3: high one tick, low two. The LEGO document does not state a duty
; (QUESTIONS.md); 1/3 is what ir_nec_tx uses for the same 38 kHz receivers.
; A mark of six cycles lasts 16 ticks from its first rise to its last fall
; (158 us less the last cycle's dark two ticks).
;
; Timing. Each carrier edge is `SETP LED, v, D` and the WAITD that lands
; it, on the exact clock edge of its tick (SEMANTICS 6.10 rule 1). The burst
; loop is the subroutine of ir_nec_tx: SETP 1 D, WAITD 2 (the rise), SETP 0
; D, WAITD 1 (the fall), DJNZ. It returns with TD at the last fall, 16 ticks
; after the first rise, so a bit of n cycles is one WAITD 3n - 18 before the
; next burst's WAITD 2 lands the next rise.
;
; Registers: r0 the host word; r1 the copies of it sent so far; r2 the
; message on the wire; r3 carrier cycles, then scratch; r4 data bits left,
; then scratch; r5 cycles to the next start; r6 (6 + 2 Ch) tm in cycles;
; r7 the bits being shifted out, then scratch.
;
; Readings taken on the open spec questions (2026-11-07): the first delay
; is (4 - Ch) tm. In the v1.20 PDF's text layer the operator is a glyph
; that does not extract ("(4  Ch)*tm"), while the "+" of the next formula,
; "(6 + 2*Ch)*tm", does; a typeset minus is the reading that fits. The
; director checked the extracted text on 2026-11-07; the printed page was
; not seen. The 6-cycle marks are shorter than the TSOP382 minimum burst,
; so a stock receiver module may not respond (see lego_pf_rx.loom).

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .thread 0
        .pins   LED = OUT0
        .tick   16                      ; the shortest tick the schedule is proved for
        .equ    TM    = 608             ; tm = 16 ms in carrier cycles
        .equ    MARKC = 6               ; carrier cycles in a mark
        .equ    BIT0  = 16              ; cycles from mark to mark: a low bit
        .equ    BIT1  = 27              ; a high bit
        .equ    BITSS = 45              ; the start and stop bits
        .equ    K     = 4               ; cycles of every gap after its loop
        .equ    LEAD  = 8               ; ticks between the decision and a start

start:  SETP    LED, 0                  ; LED off
idle:   POP     r0                      ; blocks while INQ is empty

; A new word: the LRC and its channel's spacings, then its first message
; (4 - Ch) tm + K cycles after the SETD below.
new:    MOV     r7, r0
        SHRI    r7, 4                   ; nibbles 1..3 in bits 11:0
        MOV     r3, r7
        SHRI    r3, 8                   ; nibble 1 in bits 3:0
        XOR     r7, r7, r3              ; bits 3:0: n3 ^ n1
        MOV     r3, r7
        SHRI    r3, 4                   ; bits 3:0: n2
        XOR     r7, r7, r3              ; bits 3:0: n3 ^ n2 ^ n1
        XORI    r7, 0xF
        ANDI    r7, 0xF                 ; the LRC
        MOV     r2, r0
        SHRI    r2, 4
        SHLI    r2, 4
        OR      r2, r2, r7              ; the message as it goes out
        MOV     r3, r0
        SHRI    r3, 12
        ANDI    r3, 3                   ; CC = Ch - 1; Z: channel 1
        MOV16   r5, 3 * TM              ; the first delay, (4 - Ch) tm
        MOV16   r6, 8 * TM              ; (6 + 2 Ch) tm
        BZ      ch1
        MOV16   r4, TM
chl:    SUB     r5, r5, r4              ; one channel up: tm less first,
        ADD     r6, r6, r4
        ADD     r6, r6, r4              ; 2 tm more between the later copies
        DJNZ    r3, chl
ch1:    LDI     r1, 0                   ; no copy sent yet
        SETD    1                       ; the anchor

; The gap: r5 cycles in steps of 63, then K cycles less LEAD ticks, which
; leaves TD LEAD ticks before the next start. Entered with TD at the anchor.
gap:    SUBI    r5, 63                  ; C = (r5 >= 63)
        BNC     grest
        WAITD   3 * 63
        JMP     gap
grest:  ADDI    r5, 63                  ; 0 .. 62 cycles left; Z: none
        BZ      gend
gcyc:   WAITD   3
        DJNZ    r5, gcyc
gend:   WAITD   3 * K - LEAD            ; LEAD ticks before the start

; LEAD ticks before a start: a word queued behind ends the sequence (not
; before its first copy); else this copy goes out, with r5 set to the cycles
; from its start to the next copy's.
        TEST    r1, r1
        BZ      go                      ; the first copy always goes out
        WAITB   INQ_NE, T               ; a test, not a wait: TD is reached
        BNT     pre                     ; a new word: it pre-empts the rest
go:     ADDI    r1, 1                   ; this copy's number, 1 .. 5
        MOV16   r5, 5 * TM              ; after copies 1 and 2: 5 tm
        CMPI    r1, 3
        BNC     sched                   ; r1 < 3
        MOV     r5, r6                  ; after 3 and 4: (6 + 2 Ch) tm
        CMPI    r1, 5
        BNZ     sched
        MOV     r7, r0
        SHRI    r7, 1                   ; C = HOLD
        BNC     sched                   ; the fifth without HOLD: the last
        LDI     r1, 4                   ; HOLD: every later copy is a fifth
sched:  WAITD   LEAD - 2                ; the burst's WAITD 2 lands the start

; One message, r2, most significant bit first. Entered with TD two ticks
; before the start mark's first rise; r5 = cycles from that rise to the next
; copy's, counted down as the bits go out.
msg:    LDI     r3, MARKC
        CALL    burst                   ; the start mark
        SUBI    r5, BITSS
        MOV     r7, r2
        LDI     r4, 16
        WAITD   3 * BITSS - 18          ; the start bit: 45 cycles
bit:    LDI     r3, MARKC
        CALL    burst                   ; this bit's mark
        SHLI    r7, 1                   ; C = the bit
        BC      one
        SUBI    r5, BIT0
        WAITD   3 * BIT0 - 18           ; a low bit: 16 cycles
        DJNZ    r4, bit
        JMP     stop
one:    SUBI    r5, BIT1
        WAITD   3 * BIT1 - 18           ; a high bit: 27 cycles
        DJNZ    r4, bit
stop:   LDI     r3, MARKC
        CALL    burst                   ; the stop mark; TD at its last fall
        CMPI    r1, 5
        BNZ     more
        WAITD   3 * BITSS - 18          ; the fifth: the stop bit's pause,
        JMP     idle                    ; so no message starts inside it
more:   WAITD   2                       ; TD six cycles after the stop mark's start
        SUBI    r5, MARKC + K           ; what the gap waits in its loop
        JMP     gap

; A word queued behind the sequence: the guard above saw it.
        .bounded "the WAITB above completed with T = 0, so INQ held a word in that slot's X cycle; only this thread pops INQ (SEMANTICS 6.7)"
pre:    POP     r0
        JMP     new

; The carrier: r3 cycles, each a rise, one tick high, two ticks low. Entered
; with TD two ticks before the first rise; returns with TD at the last fall.
burst:  SETP    LED, 1, D
        WAITD   2                       ; the rise lands here
        SETP    LED, 0, D
        WAITD   1                       ; the fall, one tick later
        DJNZ    r3, burst
        RET
